markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Applying Schema and Parallel Arrays

#### In Lesson 2, students build on their understanding of arrays by creating a schema and using it to design an app that handles personal data. 

<div style="background-color: #B7E0E5; border-radius: 10px; padding: 15px; margin: 20px auto; max-width: 95%; box-sizing: border-box; word-wrap: break-word; overflow-wrap: break-word;">
<p style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 12px; line-height: 120%;">💡  This is a good moment to remind students that personal data (like allergies, medications, or health information) should be handled thoughtfully and stored only for learning purposes.</p>
</div>

## Schema Design Activity

#### Students can choose to design a Food Allergy Tracker, a Medication Reminder, or create their own idea. In this professional learning session, we’ll focus on the **Food Allergy Tracker** so you can see how schema and parallel arrays work together in a full example.

## Example Schema

#### Students will start by brainstorming on their own and then use AI to help refine or expand their schema. This example schema uses the fields of allergy name, severity, symptoms, and treatment to store data. 

<img src="https://images.code.org/8946e446da983c617f39ca66ee8f578d-image-1765557163145.png" alt="sschema for allergy app: allergen name, severity, symptoms, treatment" width="450px" style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;border-radius:10px; ">

## Next up

#### Practice creating a web app for an allergy tracker in the next level. 
MARKDOWN
